# How git treats the contents of this repo, independent of anyone's local
# core.autocrlf. The point of committing this is that the answer travels with
# the code instead of living in one machine's config, where a second clone
# configured differently would rewrite the invisible character at the end of
# every line and call it a change.

# Normalise text files to LF in the repository and check them out as LF on
# every platform. Development happens on Windows, but CI and anything
# containerised are Linux, and a shell script checked out with CRLF fails there
# with "bad interpreter" -- so LF everywhere removes the translation instead of
# performing it in both directions.
#
# text=auto still leaves git to detect binary files and leave them alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
